{
  "dom_id": "question-166149",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=r(x),$ where $r(x)=\\frac{3}{5x-2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The vertical asymptote is $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall that \n\n$$\ny=r(x)=\\frac{3}{5x-2}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n5x-2=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ is the only vertical asymptote of $y=r(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14214969/assets/61d7b22d6df4c7cb85a814e84a8af2f8d162fe75d25b4e3e09258bfe965719ff.png)",
